      "grounding": "U.T. Place and J.J.C. Smart pioneered the Mind-Brain Type Identity Theory (often called Australian Materialism). A major initial objection from ordinary language philosophers was: \"A child or an ancient Greek can know they have a headache without knowing anything about neurons or the brain; therefore, headache cannot be identical to a brain process.\"\n\nTo refute this, Place introduced the crucial distinction between two senses of 'is':\n1. *The 'is' of definition*: Analytic statements true *a priori* by linguistic convention (e.g., \"A triangle is a three-sided rectilinear plane figure\").\n2. *The 'is' of composition / empirical identity*: Synthetic statements discovered *a posteriori* through scientific investigation (e.g., \"Water is H₂O\", \"Lightning is an electrical discharge\", \"A cloud is a mass of droplets\").\n\nPlace and Smart argued that mental-state terms and brain-state terms do not have the same *meaning* (sense / *Sinn*), but they share the exact same *referent* (extension / *Bedeutung*). Thus, (R) provides the definitive logical defense for (A): the identity is an empirical, synthetic discovery of science, explaining why a subject can report subjective mental states while being entirely ignorant of neurophysiology.",
