      "thinkers": "Ned Block (1978)",
      "question": "In \"Troubles with Functionalism\" (1978), Ned Block formulates the celebrated **China Brain** (or Chinese Nation) thought experiment. A billion citizens of China are organized via two-way radios and satellite communications to simulate the exact functional organization, artificial neurons, and transitional states of a human brain. What systemic dilemma does Block argue this poses for functionalism?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Functionalism is guilty of **liberalism**—it ascribes mental states and consciousness to systems that patently lack any qualitative experience (Absent Qualia).",
        "B": "Functionalism is guilty of **chauvinism**—it arbitrarily restricts consciousness only to organic, carbon-based biological organisms.",
        "C": "The China Brain demonstrates that functional transitions violate the Church-Turing thesis regarding computability.",
        "D": "The experiment proves that mental states are strictly supervenient on quantum coherent states inside neuronal microtubules."
      },
      "correct_option": "A",
      "grounding": "In \"Troubles with Functionalism\", Ned Block introduced two diagnostic terms to evaluate philosophical theories of mind:\n1. *Chauvinism*: A theory is chauvinistic if it withholds mental states from things that actually have them (e.g., Type Identity Theory, which insists pain must be C-fiber excitation, unfairly denying pain to silicon aliens or cephalopods).\n2. *Liberalism*: A theory is liberal if it attributes mental states to things that obviously lack them.\n\nBlock created the **China Brain** (or \"Homunculi-headed system\") thought experiment to prove that Functionalism is fatally *liberal*:\nSuppose the billion citizens of China simulate the functionally organized neurons of a human brain, controlling an artificial body via radio transmissions. If functionalism is true, this entire collective system must experience genuine qualitative mental states:\n> \"It has an input and an output... and between them a system of functional transitions. If the body steps on a nail, is there an entity that feels the pain? It seems absurd to suppose that the population of China, acting together as an artificial computer, would be the subject of a toothache or experience the smell of a rose.\"\n\nBlock argues that the system realizes the functional organization without having any conscious qualitative life at all (**Absent Qualia**). Thus, functionalism fails because it mistake structural causal organization for conscious phenomenal experience.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **(B) is incorrect**: Block accuses Type Physicalism of chauvinism, not functionalism; functionalism’s cardinal defect in the China Brain case is *liberalism*.\n- **(C) is incorrect**: Block explicitly grants that the China Brain is fully Turing-computable and functionally isomorphic to a human brain; his argument is ontological and phenomenological, not a mathematical challenge to the Church-Turing thesis.\n- **(D) is incorrect**: This is Roger Penrose and Stuart Hameroff’s Orchestrated Objective Reduction (Orch-OR) theory of consciousness (1994), which plays no role in Block’s 1978 critique of functionalism.",
      "reference": "Ned Block, \"Troubles with Functionalism\", in *Perception and Cognition: Issues in the Foundations of Psychology*, Minnesota Studies in the Philosophy of Science, Vol. 9, ed. C.W. Savage (University of Minnesota Press, 1978), pp. 261–325."
