      "title": "Physicalist Responses to Jackson: The Ability Hypothesis",
      "thinkers": "Laurence Nemirow (1980), David Lewis (1988, 1990)",
      "question": "In response to Frank Jackson’s Knowledge Argument, David Lewis and Laurence Nemirow formulated the **Ability Hypothesis**. How does the Ability Hypothesis preserve physicalism without denying that Mary undergoes a genuine psychological change upon seeing red for the first time?",
      "options": {
        "A": "It argues that upon release, Mary gains **practical abilities / know-how** (such as the ability to recognize, imagine, and remember red), not new **propositional knowledge / know-that** about non-physical facts.",
        "B": "It argues that Mary gains a new non-physical epiphenomenal quale that supervenes on an entirely new neural firing pattern in cortical area V4.",
        "C": "It claims that Mary’s pre-release education was simply incomplete because physicalism cannot account for quantum entanglement in visual pigments.",
        "D": "It asserts that Mary already had the perceptual experience of red in black-and-white because shades of gray contain dormant wavelengths of the visible spectrum."
      },
      "correct_option": "A",
      "grounding": "The Ability Hypothesis, formulated by Laurence Nemirow and extensively defended by David Lewis (\"What Experience Teaches\", 1988), challenges the second premise of Jackson’s syllogism: \"Upon release, Mary gains new knowledge of a fact.\"\n\nLewis draws on Gilbert Ryle's classic distinction between:\n- *Knowledge-that* (propositional knowledge: facts that can be stated in true assertions).\n- *Knowledge-how* (practical abilities, skills, and capacities: e.g., knowing how to ride a bicycle or play the piano).\n\nLewis argues that in her black-and-white room, Mary possessed *all propositional knowledge* concerning colour vision. What happens when she steps outside and sees a ripe tomato? She does not discover a new non-physical truth about the universe. Rather, she acquires a set of **practical cognitive skills**:\n1. The ability to *remember* the experience of red.\n2. The ability to *imagine* red at will.\n3. The ability to *recognize* red objects in the future without analytical deduction.\n\nBecause acquiring an ability or practical skill is not acquiring knowledge of a new non-physical fact, physicalism remains completely intact: all facts remain physical facts.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **(B) is incorrect**: Admitting that Mary gains a \"non-physical quale\" would be conceding the entire argument to Jackson's property dualism; the Ability Hypothesis strictly denies that any non-physical quale exists.\n- **(C) is incorrect**: Physicalists grant Jackson's premise that Mary's physical knowledge was complete; they attack the inference that what she gains upon release is propositional knowledge.\n- **(D) is incorrect**: This is an absurd empirical claim; black-and-white light does not contain red wavelengths, and physicalists do not dispute the novelty of Mary's sensory experience.",
      "reference": "David Lewis, \"What Experience Teaches\", *Proceedings of the Russellian Society* (1988); reprinted in *Mind and Cognition*, ed. W.G. Lycan (Blackwell, 1990), pp. 499–519; Laurence Nemirow, \"Review of Thomas Nagel's *Mortal Questions*\", *Philosophical Review* 89(3) (1980): 473–477."
