      "thinkers": "Donald Davidson (\"Mental Events\", 1970)",
      "question": "In \"Mental Events\" (1970), Donald Davidson formulated **Anomalous Monism**, reconciling the causal efficacy of the mental with the physicalist rejection of Cartesian dualism. Davidson's system rests upon three seemingly contradictory principles:\n1. *The Principle of Causal Interaction*: At least some mental events interact causally with physical events.\n2. *The Principle of the Nomological Character of Causality*: Events related as cause and effect must fall under strict deterministic laws.\n3. *The Anomalism of the Mental*: There are no strict deterministic laws on the basis of which mental events can predict, explain, or be explained by other events (no strict psychophysical or psychological laws).\n\nHow does Davidson resolve this apparent contradiction to establish **Token Identity** without type reduction?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Every individual mental event is token-identical to a physical event ($m = p$); causal relations hold between concrete individual events regardless of how they are described; when two events are causally related, there is a strict law that subsumes them under their **physical descriptions**, even though no strict laws can ever connect mental descriptions with physical descriptions due to the normative constitutive ideal of rationality governing mental vocabulary.",
        "B": "Davidson argues that mental events are made of ectoplasm that occasionally breaks through physical reality via quantum anomalies.",
        "C": "He resolves the contradiction by proving that physical laws are illusions invented by physicists to secure university research grants.",
        "D": "Davidson claims that mental events never cause anything and that human actions are complete hallucinations."
      },
      "correct_option": "A",
      "grounding": "Davidson’s Anomalous Monism is one of the most brilliant solutions to the mind-body problem in 20th-century analytic philosophy:\n1. **The Trilemma**:\n   - If mental events cause physical events (Principle 1), and causation requires strict deterministic laws (Principle 2), it seems there must be strict psychophysical laws ($\\text{Mental State } M \\to \\text{Physical State } P$).\n   - But Principle 3 denies that such laws exist! How can all three be true?\n2. **Davidson's Dual-Vocabulary Ontology**:\n   - Davidson distinguishes between **events themselves** (concrete particulars in spacetime) and **descriptions of events**.\n   - Causation is an extensional relation between events: event $c$ causes event $e$. This holds no matter how we describe them.\n   - Laws are intensional/linguistic: an event is subsumed under a law only under a specific vocabulary or description.\n3. **The Constitutive Ideal of Rationality**:\n   - Physical vocabulary is governed by constitutive principles of measurement, spatio-temporal continuity, and physical conservation laws.\n   - Mental vocabulary (beliefs, desires, intentions) is governed by the **Constitutive Ideal of Rationality** (coherence, consistency, norms of rationality, and the Principle of Charity).\n   - Because the two domains operate under incommensurable constitutive criteria, there can be **no bridge laws** linking mental predicates to physical predicates (no type-type identity).\n4. **The Monist Synthesis (Token Identity)**:\n   - When my desire to drink causes my arm to reach, that specific mental event *is* a specific physical event (token identity).\n   - Under its physical description (\"neuronal spike train in motor cortex\"), it is subsumed under a strict physical law.\n   - Under its mental description (\"desire for water\"), it is anomalous (free from strict deterministic laws). Thus: **Monism** (all events are physical), but **Anomalous** (mental types cannot be reduced to physical types).",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **(B) is incorrect**: Davidson is an uncompromising physicalist monist; he rejects Cartesian ectoplasm, vitalism, and dualism completely.\n- **(C) is incorrect**: Physical laws are strictly real and deterministic in Davidson's framework (Principle of the Nomological Character of Causality).\n- **(D) is incorrect**: Principle 1 explicitly affirms that mental events are causally efficacious in producing physical bodily actions.",
      "reference": "Donald Davidson, \"Mental Events\", in *Experience and Theory*, ed. L. Foster and J. W. Swanson (University of Massachusetts Press, 1970), reprinted in *Essays on Actions and Events* (Oxford University Press, 1980), pp. 207–227."
