      "grounding": "Burge's target is **Individualism** (the classical Cartesian/Internalist doctrine that an agent's mental states are entirely determined by what lies within their physical body and skull):\n1. **The Actual World Scenario**:\n   - Bert has a pain in his thigh. He goes to his doctor and says: *\"I have arthritis in my thigh.\"*\n   - In our actual English-speaking world, \"arthritis\" is defined medically strictly as an inflammation of the **joints**.\n   - Bert has a false belief: he believes that *arthritis* has spread to his thigh. His mental state contains the concept **arthritis**, which he partially misunderstands.\n2. **The Counterfactual Scenario**:\n   - Imagine a counterfactual world where Bert’s physical history, internal brain states, non-intentional behaviors, and bodily atoms are **molecule-for-molecule identical** to the actual Bert.\n   - However, in this counterfactual society, the medical community uses the word \"thirtritis\" (pronounced or spelled identically) to cover *both* joint inflammation and muscle/thigh inflammation.\n   - When counterfactual Bert says *\"I have thirtritis in my thigh\"*, his belief is **true**! And his belief does not contain the concept of arthritis (since arthritis as defined in our world does not exist in their language).\n3. **The Philosophical Verdict**:\n   - Actual Bert and Counterfactual Bert are **physically, neurophysiologically, and internally identical**.\n   - Yet they have **different beliefs** with different propositional contents!\n   - Therefore, what belief an agent has is not determined solely by internal physical brain states, but is constitutively dependent upon the **social-linguistic practices of the wider linguistic community**. Mentality is social and externalist.",
