      "grounding": "McGinn's New Mysterianism offers a naturalistic, non-magical explanation for why the mind-body problem has resisted centuries of brilliant philosophy:\n1. **The Existence of a Natural Solution**:\n   - McGinn is a naturalist: there is nothing supernatural or miraculous about consciousness. There is some natural, objective physical-ontological property $\\mathcal{P}$ of the brain that accounts for how conscious states arise from physical tissue.\n2. **The Principle of Cognitive Closure (Chomsky/McGinn)**:\n   - A type of mind $\\mathcal{M}$ is **cognitively closed** with respect to a property or theory $\\mathcal{T}$ if the concept-forming procedures of $\\mathcal{M}$ cannot in principle grasp $\\mathcal{T}$.\n   - A monkey can never grasp the Riemann Hypothesis or general relativity; this is not an embarrassment for physics, but a natural fact of primate evolutionary biology.\n3. **The Epistemic Dilemma of the Human Mind**:\n   - Humans have only two avenues to investigate reality:\n     1. *Introspection*: Gives us direct access to phenomenal conscious states (pain, color, thought), but introspection gives us **zero information about physical brain tissue, neurons, or spatial properties**.\n     2. *Perception & Physical Science*: Gives us access to the physical world, but physical perception is **strictly spatial** (shapes, sizes, electrical potentials, neurochemical synapses).\n   - The mystery property $\\mathcal{P}$ must bridge spatial brain states with non-spatial phenomenal qualia.\n   - But we cannot reach $\\mathcal{P}$ from introspection (it reveals no brain properties), and we cannot reach $\\mathcal{P}$ from spatial science (looking inside the skull reveals only spatial arrangements of matter, never qualia).\n   - Therefore, the human cognitive architecture is structurally blind to $\\mathcal{P}$. The mind-body problem is a mystery to us, not because it is magic, but because human brains did not evolve the sensory or conceptual equipment to understand their own generation of consciousness.",
