      "correct_option": "A",
      "grounding": "McDowell's *Mind and World* is widely considered one of the most important philosophical texts of the late 20th century, attempting to rescue empirical realism from an intolerable oscillation:\n1. **Horn 1: The Myth of the Given (Sellars 1956)**:\n   - Foundationalism tries to ground knowledge in raw, pre-conceptual sensory inputs (the \"Given\").\n   - *Sellars/McDowell refutation*: A raw causal impact of light on the retina is merely an event in the **\"realm of law\"** (mechanical nature). A physical cause cannot serve as a **justification or reason** for a belief. Causes produce effects; only propositions in the space of reasons can justify beliefs. Conflating causal impact with epistemic justification is the Myth of the Given.\n2. **Horn 2: Coherentism (Davidson / Rorty)**:\n   - To avoid the Given, coherentists claim that *\"nothing can count as a reason for holding a belief except another belief.\"*\n   - *McDowell's refutation*: If beliefs are only justified by other beliefs within an enclosed linguistic web, human thought loses all objective contact with the real world. Mind becomes **\"a frictionless spinning in a void\"**, detached from physical reality.\n3. **McDowell's Kantian Solution**:\n   - Revives Kant's dictum: *\"Thoughts without content are empty; intuitions without concepts are blind.\"*\n   - McDowell argues that in human adult perception, **conceptual capacities are already active in perceptual experience itself**.\n   - Perception is not a two-stage process (first a raw dumb sensation, then a conceptual judgment slapped onto it). Rather, our visual experience is *already conceptualized*: we passively see *that the wall is yellow*.\n   - Because experience is already conceptual, it can stand in rational, justificatory relations to our beliefs (providing epistemic friction), while remaining receptive to the world. The Space of Reasons reaches all the way to the boundary of our sensory receptivity.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **(B) is incorrect**: McDowell is an empirical realist who insists that nature provides real epistemic friction on human thought; he explicitly rejects Rortyan anti-realism and linguistic idealism.\n- **(C) is incorrect**: A literal, humorous spatial misinterpretation of Sellars's epistemological phrase \"the logical space of reasons\".\n- **(D) is incorrect**: McDowell is an analytical philosopher synthesizing Aristotle, Kant, and Frege to defend rational empiricism, not medieval scholasticism.",
      "reference": "John McDowell, *Mind and World* (Harvard University Press, 1994), Lectures I and II; Wilfrid Sellars, *Empiricism and the Philosophy of Mind* (Harvard University Press, 1956/1997)."
