      "grounding": "Galen Strawson takes physicalism more seriously than traditional reductionists:\n1. **Real Physicalism Must Acknowledge the Hardest Fact**:\n   - Strawson defines \"Real Physicalism\" as the claim that every real concrete phenomenon is physical.\n   - The most undeniable, directly known concrete phenomenon in the universe is **experiential consciousness** (*qualia*, feeling).\n   - Therefore, a genuine physicalist cannot deny that experience is real and physical.\n2. **The Impossibility of Radical Emergence**:\n   - Standard materialists claim that consciousness is an \"emergent property\" of complex brains, just as liquidity is an emergent property of $H_2O$ molecules.\n   - *Strawson's Critique*: Liquidity is **intelligible emergence**: liquidity is simply the spatial-kinetic behavior of non-rigid molecular bonds sliding past each other. The micro-properties ($H_2O$ molecules, dipole attraction) conceptually and physically constitute the macro-property (liquidity).\n   - In contrast, experientiality emerging from totally non-experiential matter would be **Radical / Brutal Emergence**: getting something completely new out of a domain that contains zero trace of it.\n   - Strawson argues: *\"You cannot get experientiality out of non-experientiality. It is as impossible as getting spatial extension out of something that has no spatial properties whatsoever.\"* If you start with 100% dead, unfeeling, unconscious matter, no amount of complex mechanical rearrangement can suddenly burst into subjective feeling. That would be creation *ex nihilo* (magic).\n3. **The Conclusion**:\n   - If radical emergence is impossible, and physicalism is true, and experience exists, then **experience must have been present at the bottom level of physical reality all along**.\n   - The ultimate physical components of the universe (the fundamental fields, particles, or energy) are experiential in their intrinsic nature. Hence: **Physicalism entails Panpsychism**.",
