      "question": "In \"Inverted Earth\" (1990), Ned Block crafted an intricate thought experiment to refute **Strong Representationalism / Intentionalism** (the view held by Michael Tye and Fred Dretske that the phenomenal character of an experience is strictly identical to its intentional/representational content). In Block's thought experiment, you are unconsciously transported to Inverted Earth and fitted with color-inverting lenses. How does your long-term adaptation to Inverted Earth prove that phenomenal character cannot be identical to representational content?",
