      "thinkers": "Lawrence Weiskrantz (*Blindsight: A Case Study and Implications*, 1986), Nicholas Humphrey",
      "question": "In his pioneering neuropsychological investigations of patient **D.B.**, Oxford neuroscientist Lawrence Weiskrantz discovered the profound phenomenon of **Blindsight** resulting from damage to the **primary visual cortex (striate cortex / Area V1 / Brodmann Area 17)**. What is the defining empirical paradox of blindsight, and what does it reveal about the architecture of visual perception?",
      "options": {
        "A": "When visual stimuli are presented inside the patient's clinically blind visual field (scotoma), the patient sincerely insists that they consciously see nothing whatsoever; yet, when forced to guess the stimulus's location, movement direction, or line orientation, they perform with extraordinary accuracy (up to 90–95%), proving that unconscious sensory information processing can be completely dissociated from subjective phenomenal visual consciousness.",
        "B": "Blindsight patients are completely blind in their eyes, but can read printed text using sensory tactile receptors located in their fingertips.",
        "C": "The experiment proves that the human eye directly projects images onto the pineal gland.",
        "D": "Blindsight patients have supernatural telepathic vision that allows them to see through solid concrete walls."
      },
      "correct_option": "A",
      "grounding": "Weiskrantz's discovery of blindsight in 1974 represents one of the most celebrated double dissociations in cognitive neuroscience:\n1. **The Anatomical Basis**:\n   - Normal conscious visual perception travels through the **geniculostriate pathway**:\n     $$\\text{Retina} \\longrightarrow \\text{Lateral Geniculate Nucleus (LGN)} \\longrightarrow \\text{Primary Visual Cortex (V1 / Striate)} \\longrightarrow \\text{Extrastriate Areas (V2, V4, MT/V5)}$$\n   - When Area V1 is destroyed (due to stroke, trauma, or surgery), the patient suffers cortical blindness in the corresponding part of the visual field (a **scotoma**). The patient has zero subjective phenomenal experience of sight: it is not black; it is a total experiential void (like what normal humans \"see\" behind their heads).\n2. **The Subcortical Tectopulvinar Pathway**:\n   - A secondary, phylogenetically older visual pathway bypasses V1 entirely:\n     $$\\text{Retina} \\longrightarrow \\text{Superior Colliculus} \\longrightarrow \\text{Pulvinar Nucleus of Thalamus} \\longrightarrow \\text{Parietal / Motor Areas}$$\n3. **The Philosophical Paradox**:\n   - In forced-choice testing, when light targets are flashed in their blind field, patient D.B. would say: *\"I cannot see anything; I am just guessing blindly.\"*\n   - Yet his \"guesses\" pointed to the target with 95% accuracy! He could discriminate horizontal from vertical lines, identify the direction of moving dots, and even discriminate emotional facial expressions (affective blindsight).\n4. **Epistemological Consequence**:\n   - Traditional philosophy assumed that perceiving an object requires being consciously aware of it.\n   - Blindsight proves that **information processing, spatial detection, and behavioral guidance can execute entirely in the dark**, without any phenomenal qualia. Primary visual cortex (V1) is necessary for phenomenal visual consciousness, but not necessary for all unconscious visual computations.",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **(B) is incorrect**: Finger reading is Braille tactile perception; blindsight is mediated by the patient's physical eyes and subcortical ocular pathways (superior colliculus).\n- **(C) is incorrect**: The visual pathway projects to the occipital lobe, not Descartes's pineal gland.\n- **(D) is incorrect**: Blindsight operates strictly within normal physical optical laws and ocular refraction; there is zero supernatural or telepathic mechanism.",
      "reference": "Lawrence Weiskrantz, *Blindsight: A Case Study and Implications* (Oxford University Press, 1986); Lawrence Weiskrantz, *Consciousness Lost and Found: A Neuropsychological Exploration* (Oxford University Press, 1997)."
