      "correct_option": "A",
      "grounding": "These two syndromes provide extraordinary clinical evidence for the modular dissociation of **sensory processing**, **phenomenal experience**, and **epistemic metacognition (belief-monitoring)**:\n1. **Anton-Babinski Syndrome (Visual Anosognosia)**:\n   - First described by Austrian neurologist Gabriel Anton (1899) and Joseph Babinski.\n   - Cause: Extensive bilateral damage to the visual cortex (occipital lobes), often due to posterior cerebral artery stroke.\n   - The patient is **completely, physically blind**. They cannot see an elephant in front of them and bump into walls.\n   - *The Epistemic Horror*: The patient **firmly denies that they are blind**! If asked what color the doctor's tie is, they immediately confabulate: *\"It is a lovely dark blue tie with red stripes.\"* If asked why they bumped into the table, they say: *\"The lighting in this room is terribly dim today.\"*\n   - Neurophilosophical meaning: The brain's visual input channels are destroyed, but the higher-order visual monitoring circuits in the parietal/frontal lobes fail to receive the signal that vision has ceased. The brain's generative prediction engine continues confabulating an internal world and accepts it as reality.\n2. **Charles Bonnet Syndrome (CBS)**:\n   - First described by Swiss naturalist Charles Bonnet (1760), observing his 87-year-old grandfather who was nearly blind from severe cataracts.\n   - Cause: Peripheral ocular damage (macular degeneration, cataracts, glaucoma) with an intact, healthy visual cortex.\n   - Due to visual deafferentation (sensory deprivation), the starved visual cortex becomes hyper-excitable and begins spontaneously firing, generating **vivid, complex visual hallucinations**: colorful figures in 18th-century costumes, floating miniature patterns (lilliputian hallucinations), buildings, and animals.\n   - *The Crucial Contrast*: Patients with CBS **know their visions are not real**! They retain perfect reality testing: *\"I know there isn't really a medieval knight standing in my kitchen; my eyes are playing tricks on me.\"*\n3. **The Philosophical Double Dissociation**:\n   - Anton's Syndrome: Blindness + Delusional belief of vision (Metacognitive failure).\n   - Charles Bonnet Syndrome: Hallucinatory qualia + Rational knowledge of blindness (Metacognitive preservation).",
      "distractor_analysis": "- **(B) is incorrect**: Both syndromes are well-documented clinical conditions in adult humans (most common in elderly neurology patients).\n- **(C) is incorrect**: Drug allergies and simple sleep deprivation do not cause the structural occipital cortical lesions of Anton's syndrome or the deafferentation release of Charles Bonnet syndrome.\n- **(D) is incorrect**: Anton patients cannot navigate physical space, and CBS hallucinations do not represent real physical objects; neither provides veridical vision without eyes.",
      "reference": "Gabriel Anton, \"Über die Selbstwahrnehmung der Herderkrankungen des Gehirns durch den Kranken\", *Archiv für Psychiatrie und Nervenkrankheiten* 32 (1899): 86–127; Charles Bonnet, *Essai analytique sur les facultés de l'âme* (1760); Dominic H. ffytche et al., \"The Anatomy of Conscious Vision: An fMRI Study of Visual Hallucinations\", *Nature Neuroscience* 1, no. 8 (1998): 738–742."
